  "caveats": [
    "This is the idealised case: Gaussian noise, no deconvolution error, no primary-beam or bandpass systematic, and the true source spectrum assumed to be a power law across the band. Real Taylor-term alpha will be no better than this and probably worse, so a source failing here cannot be rescued by real data.",
    "Bias is negligible above S/N ~20 with the T0 > 5 sigma guard, but grows below it, and near S/N ~6 the guard itself selects upward fluctuations of T0 and shrinks |alpha| -- a selection effect, not noise.",
    "sigma_alpha here is the statistical term only. Pulse dilution (the pulse occupies a fraction of the integration) is a separate systematic and is not folded in."
  ],
